===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|en|enm|bog}} (originally chiefly in Ireland and Scotland), from {{der|en|ga|-}} and {{der|en|gd|bogach||soft, boggy ground}}, from {{der|en|sga|bog||soft}},<ref name=oed>''Oxford English Dictionary'', 1st ed. "bog, ''n.¹''" & "bog, ''v.¹''" Oxford University Press (Oxford), 1887.</ref> from {{der|en|cel-pro|*buggos||soft, tender}} + {{der|en|sga|-ach}}, from {{der|en|cel-pro|*-ākos}}.

The frequent use to form compounds regarding the animals and plants in such areas mimics Irish compositions such as {{m|ga|bog-luachair||[[bulrush]], [[bogrush]]}}.<ref name="oed"/>

Its use for toilets is now often derived from the resemblance of latrines and outhouse cesspools to bogholes,<ref>''Oxford Dictionaries''. "British English: [https://web.archive.org/web/20160329085311/http://www.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/english/bog bog]". Oxford University Press (Oxford), 2016.</ref><ref name=collins>''The Collins English Dictionary''. "[http://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/bog bog]". HarperCollins (London), 2016.</ref> but the noun sense appears to be a clipped form of {{m|en|boghouse||[[outhouse]], [[privy]]}},<ref>''Oxford English Dictionary'', "bog, ''n.⁴''"</ref> which derived (possibly via {{m|en|boggard}}) from the verb ''to bog'',<ref>''Oxford English Dictionary'', "'bog-house, ''n.''" & "† 'boggard, ''n.²''".</ref> still used in Australian English.<ref name=collins/> The derivation and its connection to other senses of "bog" remains uncertain, however, owing to an extreme lack of early citations due to its perceived vulgarity.<ref>''Merriam-Webster Online''. "[http://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/bog bog]". Merriam-Webster (Springfield, Mass.), 2016.</ref><ref name=oedthebog>''Oxford English Dictionary'', "bog, ''v.³''"</ref>

